url = maybeAppendQueryString(url);
HttpGet method = new HttpGet(url);
As you can see from this code snippet, the full path of the target URL is taken from the user input (URL path).
Another problem is that this SSRF request will reuse the same httpContext from the artifact requests. Because of that, if the current proxy repository has authentication enabled, Nexus will submit user credentials to the attacker controlled server if the server asks for them.
Vulnerable code location: https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/org/sonatype/nexus/plugins/nexus-rrb-plugin/2.15.2-03/nexus-rrb-plugin-2.15.2-03-sources.jar
Path inside archive: /nexus-rrb-plugin-2.15.2-03-sources/org/sonatype/nexus/plugins/rrb/RemoteBrowserResource.java
Impact
This vulnerability allows an unauthenticated attacker to send arbitrary HTTP GET requests to any URL, potentially leaking sensitive credentials. If the server is configured to use basic authentication for the current proxy repository, the credentials will be leaked to the external host. Note that the Remote Browser Plugin is enabled by default in Nexus 2.
