Summary
WooCommerce 10.4.2 contains a vulnerability (GHSL-2025-129) where a logged-in customer can access and view details of any guest user’s order, potentially exposing sensitive order information.
Project
WooCommerce
Tested Version
10.4.2
Details
Logged in customer can view any guest order via REST API ( GHSL-2025-129 )
The Store API allows any logged-in user to pass is_authorized() for guest orders because current_user_can(pay_for_order, $order_id) is true for orders with no associated user (see wc_customer_has_capability ):
function wc_customer_has_capability ( $allcaps , $caps , $args ) {
if ( isset ( $caps [ 0 ] ) ) {
switch ( $caps [ 0 ] ) {
...
case 'pay_for_order' :
$user_id = intval ( $args [ 1 ] );
$order_id = isset ( $args [ 2 ] ) ? $args [ 2 ] : null ;
...
$order = wc_get_order ( $order_id );
if ( $order && ( $user_id === $order -> get_user_id () || ! $order -> get_user_id () ) ) {
$allcaps [ 'pay_for_order' ] = true ;
}
break ;
For logged-in users ( get_current_user_id() != 0 below), is_authorized does not perform order_key or billing_email validation:
public function is_authorized ( \WP_REST_Request $request ) {
...
try {
...
if ( get_current_user_id () === 0 ) {
$this -> order_controller -> validate_order_key ( $order_id , $order_key );
$this -> validate_billing_email_matches_order ( $order_id , $billing_email );
}
This allows access to arbitrary guest orders by just using the order_id instead of the need of knowing the corresponding order_key . Unlike order_key , the order_id is easy to guess and enumerate, and a failure will only result in a 403 error. This significantly increases the risk of disclosing order details of guest users, including addresses, name and phone number.
