GitHub Security Lab advisory GHSL-2026-102: Unauthorized exfiltration of decrypted attachments in Signal through Intent redirection (April 3, 2026)
Coordinated Disclosure Timeline
2026-03-28: Security at signal.org was notified about the vulnerability
2026-03-31: Signal team has informed that the fix was committed and the website apk was updated to v8.4.2
Summary
Versions >= v6.38.0 and < v8.4.2 of the website flavor of Signal for Android (distributed at https://signal.org/android/apk ) allow another installed app without any permissions to exfiltrate decrypted attachments (photos, videos, documents, voice notes) by exploiting Intent redirection, potentially exposing sensitive user data.
Project
Signal-Android
Tested Version
v8.4.1
Details
Decrypted attachments exfiltration through Intent redirection ( GHSL-2026-102 )
Signal distributes its Android app through multiple channels:
play - Google Play Store. Most users get it here. Google handles updates.
website - Downloaded directly from https://signal.org/android/apk. For users who don’t have or don’t want to use Google Play (e.g., degoogled phones, some countries where Play isn’t available, privacy-conscious users who avoid Google services).
github - GitHub releases. Transparency. Reproducible builds.
Signal’s website build handles its own APK updates (since there’s no Play Store to do it). Instead, it polls https://updates.signal.org/android/latest.json for new versions, downloads the APK itself, and uses Android’s PackageInstaller API to install it - which is exactly why ApkUpdatePackageInstallerReceiver exists and is exported in that variant. The Play Store and GitHub builds do not register this receiver.
The attack chain involves four components: an unprotected broadcast receiver, an unsanitized intent relaunch, a content provider that serves decrypted data, and a no-op access check in the default configuration.
Part 1: The Entry Point - An Exported Receiver With No Protection
To receive callbacks from Android’s PackageInstaller , website build registers a BroadcastReceiver in its AndroidManifest.xml :
<receiver
android:name= ".apkupdate.ApkUpdatePackageInstallerReceiver"
android:exported= "true" />
Three things are missing here:
No android:permission - any app can send it a broadcast, no special permission required
No <intent-filter> - the receiver accepts any explicit intent targeting it
No sender verification - the code inside never checks who sent the broadcast
This means any app installed on the same device - even one with zero declared permissions - can craft an intent and trigger this receiver.
Part 2: The Vulnerable Code Path - Unsanitized Intent Relaunch
When the broadcast arrives, the receiver’s onReceive() method reads the status code from the intent:
override fun onReceive ( context : Context , intent : Intent ?) {
val statusCode : Int = intent ?. getIntExtra ( PackageInstaller . EXTRA_STATUS , - 1 ) ?: - 1
val userInitiated = intent ?. getBooleanExtra ( EXTRA_USER_INITIATED , false ) ?: false
when ( statusCode ) {
// ...
PackageInstaller . STATUS_PENDING_USER_ACTION ->
handlePendingUserAction ( context , userInitiated , intent !! )
// ...
}
}
The default value for EXTRA_STATUS when the extra is absent is -1 , which matches STATUS_PENDING_USER_ACTION , routing execution into handlePendingUserAction() .
Inside handlePendingUserAction() :
private fun handlePendingUserAction ( context : Context , userInitiated : Boolean , intent : Intent ) {
val downloadId = intent . getLongExtra ( EXTRA_DOWNLOAD_ID , - 2 )
if (! userInitiated ) {
ApkUpdateNotifications . showInstallPrompt ( context , downloadId )
return // ← safe path: just shows a notification
}
val promptIntent : Intent ? = intent . getParcelableExtraCompat (
Intent . EXTRA_INTENT , Intent :: class . java
)
if ( promptIntent == null ) {
ApkUpdateNotifications . showInstallPrompt ( context , downloadId )
return
}
promptIntent . apply {
putExtra ( Intent . EXTRA_NOT_UNKNOWN_SOURCE , true )
putExtra ( Intent . EXTRA_INSTALLER_PACKAGE_NAME , "com.android.vending" )
addFlags ( Intent . FLAG_ACTIVITY_NEW_TASK )
}
context . startActivity ( promptIntent ) // ← launches attacker's intent
}
There are three problems here:
The userInitiated guard is trivially bypassed. The value is read directly from the incoming broadcast ( EXTRA_USER_INITIATED ). An attacker simply sets it to true .
The inner intent is completely attacker-controlled. The getParcelableExtraCompat() call is just a compatibility wrapper around getParcelableExtra() - it performs no validation:
fun < T : Parcelable > Intent . getParcelableExtraCompat ( key : String , clazz : Class < T >): T ? {
return if ( Build . VERSION . SDK_INT >= 33 ) {
this . getParcelableExtra ( key , clazz )
} else {
this . getParcelableExtra ( key )
}
}
Whatever intent the attacker packed under Intent.EXTRA_INTENT is extracted as-is: target component, data URIs, ClipData, and flags are all preserved.
addFlags() preserves the attacker’s flags. The code uses addFlags(Intent.FLAG_ACTIVITY_NEW_TASK) , which performs a bitwise OR . If the attacker already set FLAG_GRANT_READ_URI_PERMISSION on the inner intent, that flag survives. Had the code used setFlags() (which replaces all flags), the attacker’s grant flag would have been overwritten.
The final context.startActivity(promptIntent) executes under Signal’s UID . The Android system sees Signal - the owner of its own content providers - requesting an activity launch with URI grants attached. The system honors the request.
Part 3: The Target - Signal’s Attachment Provider
The destination of those URI grants is Signal’s PartProvider , declared in AndroidManifest.xml :
<provider
android:name= ".providers.PartProvider"
android:authorities= "${applicationId}.part"
android:exported= "false"
android:grantUriPermissions= "true" />
While exported="false" prevents direct access from other apps, grantUriPermissions="true" allows Signal’s own process to delegate per-URI read access to another app - which is exactly what FLAG_GRANT_READ_URI_PERMISSION does when attached to an intent with ClipData containing content://org.thoughtcrime.securesms.part/part/<id> URIs.
When the receiving app opens such a URI, PartProvider.openFile() is called:
public ParcelFileDescriptor openFile ( @NonNull Uri uri , @NonNull String mode )
throws FileNotFoundException {
if ( KeyCachingService . isLocked ( getContext ())) {
Log . w ( TAG , "masterSecret was null, abandoning." );
return null ;
}
// ...
if ( uriMatcher . match ( uri ) == SINGLE_ROW ) {
final PartUriParser partUri = new PartUriParser ( uri );
return getParcelStreamProxyForAttachment ( partUri . getPartId ());
}
throw new FileNotFoundException ( "Request for bad part." );
}
This returns fully decrypted attachment bytes. The only access check is isLocked() .
The attachment IDs themselves are trivially enumerable. PartUriParser simply extracts the trailing numeric segment:
class PartUriParser ( private val uri : Uri ) {
val partId : AttachmentId
get () = AttachmentId ( id )
private val id : Long
get () = ContentUris . parseId ( uri )
}
And AttachmentId is just a sequential database row ID:
data class AttachmentId (
@JsonProperty ( "rowId" )
@JvmField
val id : Long
)
URIs follow the pattern content://org.thoughtcrime.securesms.part/part/1 , .../part/2 , .../part/3 , and so on. An attacker can enumerate the entire attachment table.
Part 4: The Lock Check
The sole remaining defense is KeyCachingService.isLocked() :
public static synchronized boolean isLocked ( Context context ) {
boolean locked = masterSecret == null
&& (! SignalStore . settings (). getPassphraseDisabled ()
|| SignalStore . settings (). getScreenLockEnabled ());
return locked ;
}
The two settings have these defaults in SettingsValues.java:
public boolean getPassphraseDisabled () {
return getBoolean ( PASSPHRASE_DISABLED , true ); // default: true (passphrase IS disabled)
}
public boolean getScreenLockEnabled () {
return getBoolean ( SCREEN_LOCK_ENABLED , false ); // default: false (screen lock NOT enabled)
}
Substituting the defaults into the formula:
locked = (masterSecret == null) && (!true || false)
= (masterSecret == null) && (false || false)
= (masterSecret == null) && false
= false
Regardless of the masterSecret state, the expression always evaluates to false in the default configuration. The isLocked() check evaluates to false for the vast majority of Signal users who have not manually enabled the app’s screen lock feature under Settings > Privacy.
Putting It Together
The complete attack chain:
A zero-permission app sends a broadcast to the exported receiver
Missing EXTRA_STATUS defaults to -1 (= STATUS_PENDING_USER_ACTION )
Attacker-supplied signal.user_initiated = true bypasses the notification fallback
An attacker-crafted inner intent is extracted without sanitization
addFlags() preserves the attacker’s FLAG_GRANT_READ_URI_PERMISSION
Signal’s process launches the attacker’s activity with URI grants for PartProvider
The attacker’s activity opens each granted URI, receiving decrypted attachment bytes
isLocked() returns false in the default configuration, providing no defense
Mitigations
Platform mitigation : On Android 10+ (SDK 29+), Background Activity Launch (BAL) restrictions block startActivity() from a broadcast receiver when the calling app has no visible activity. This prevents the attack on modern devices. However, Signal supports devices down to Android 6.0 ( minSdk = 23 ), and devices running Android 6–9 have no BAL protection.
Nevertheless , devices running Android 10+ (SDK 29+) are still vulnerable, because a malicious app may bypass BAL either by bringing Signal to the foreground via startActivity with Signal’s launch intent, or by broadcasting the request periodically and waiting until the user activates Signal.
App-level mitigation : Users who have manually enabled Signal’s screen lock (Settings > Privacy > Screen lock) are protected, since isLocked() would return true and PartProvider.openFile() would return null .
Impact
Full read access to EVERY decrypted attachment in Signal’s encrypted database:
All photos and videos received from contacts
All documents and files
All voice notes
All stickers, GIFs
Attachments from disappearing messages still on disk
View-once media (not yet viewed)
The same primitive also allows launching any exported=false Signal activity (ConversationActivity, WebRtcCallActivity, etc.) though the URI-grant exfiltration is the highest-impact use.
CWEs
CWE-668: “Exposure of Resource to Wrong Sphere”
Credit
This issue was discovered with the GitHub Security Lab Taskflow Agent and verified by GHSL team member @JarLob (Jaroslav Lobačevski) .
Contact
You can contact the GHSL team at securitylab@github.com , please include a reference to GHSL-2026-102 in any communication regarding this issue.

GitHub Security Lab advisory GHSL-2026-112_GHSL-2026-113: Cross-thread authorization flaws allow unauthorized pin changes and poll votes in Signal for iOS (September 21, 2026)
Coordinated Disclosure Timeline
2026-04-10: Security at signal.org was notified about the vulnerability.
2026-04-15: Signal security informed that there was collision with another report.
2026-04-15: 8.7.0.1523 with the fix was released.
Summary
Signal-iOS contains vulnerabilities in cross-thread authorization for pinning/unpinning messages ( >= 7.91 ) and poll votes ( >= 7.84 ).
Signal for iOS validates a dataMessage sender’s group membership against the thread named by the envelope’s group context, but the Pinned Message and Poll Vote handlers then look up the target message in a global, thread-blind index. The validated thread is never compared to the thread of the message that gets mutated.
Any registered Signal user can therefore unpin a message in any group on the recipient’s device, corrupt that group’s pin record, or cast a vote in a poll in a group they are not a member of — provided they can name the target by (timestamp, authorAci) . The poll-vote handler has no message-request consultation at all. The pin/unpin handler does consult hasPendingMessageRequest , but a freshly-created TSContactThread defaults to shouldThreadBeVisible = false , which short-circuits that check before it reaches the whitelist — so an attacker who has never previously contacted the recipient passes the gate. Only an attacker whose 1:1 thread is already in the visible-but-unaccepted state is blocked.
The impact is local to the targeted recipient’s database (the malicious dataMessage is delivered 1:1, not to the group fan-out, so other group members are unaffected) and does not touch message content, keys, or trust state. The author-binding checks themselves are correct: the attacker can only unpin/vote as themselves , not impersonate another principal.
Root cause: InteractionStore.fetchMessage is not thread-scoped
SignalServiceKit/Messages/Interactions/InteractionStore.swift:251-280
public func fetchMessage (
timestamp : UInt64 ,
incomingMessageAuthor : Aci ?,
transaction : DBReadTransaction ,
) throws -> TSMessage ? {
let records = try InteractionRecord . fetchAll (
transaction . database ,
sql : """
SELECT *
FROM \( InteractionRecord . databaseTableName )
WHERE \( interactionColumn : . timestamp ) = ?
""" ,
arguments : [ timestamp ],
)
for record in records {
if incomingMessageAuthor == nil , let outgoingMessage = try TSInteraction . fromRecord ( record ) as? TSOutgoingMessage {
return outgoingMessage
}
if
let incomingMessage = try TSInteraction . fromRecord ( record ) as? TSIncomingMessage ,
let authorUUID = incomingMessage . authorUUID ,
try ServiceId . parseFrom ( serviceIdString : authorUUID ) == incomingMessageAuthor
{
return incomingMessage
}
}
return nil
}
The query has a single predicate ( timestamp = ? ) and the post-fetch loop filters only by author. Both timestamp and incomingMessageAuthor are taken directly from sender-supplied protobuf fields by every caller below.
This contrasts with the older InteractionFinder.findMessage(withTimestamp:threadId:author:) used by Reactions, Remote Delete, and Admin Delete — that path does require message.uniqueThreadId == threadId and is not affected.
Project
Signal-iOS
Tested Version
v8.6
Details
Issue 1: Cross-thread pin/unpin ( GHSL-2026-112 )
1.1 — The membership check is bound to the envelope’s thread, not the target’s thread
SignalServiceKit/Messages/MessageReceiver.swift:1022-1030
private func preprocessDataMessage (
